# Direction of picture and sound

## Picture

**Style.** A painted storybook brought to life: soft gouache-and-watercolour illustration, visible paper grain and brush texture, gentle painterly light, subtle 2.5D motion (falling snow, flickering flame, slow camera moves). Not photoreal, not 3D-plastic, not anime. Think of a classic 19th-century Danish fairy-tale illustration animated with restraint. One look for every shot: the production designer's style frame is the reference for all key frames and shots.

**World.** A small Danish town, 1840s, New Year's Eve: tall narrow gabled houses in ochre, rust and grey stucco, small-paned windows lit by candles (no electric light, no gas lamps), snow-covered cobbles, dark sky with falling snow. The CORNER is a nook where two house fronts meet, one standing further out than the other; rough, cold, plastered wall the colour of wet stone.

**The girl.** About 8 years old, thin, pale face with red-cold nose and cheeks, large eyes; long fair hair in loose curls, bareheaded; a thin, patched brown-grey wool dress; an old faded blue apron, its corners knotted up to hold matches; a small bundle of matches in her hand; bare feet, red and blue with cold (in shot 2 only, her mother's far-too-big worn slippers). She wears the same clothes in every shot, including the visions; in the ascent (shot 16) her figure is bathed in light but still recognisably her.

**The grandmother.** Old, small, kind lined face, white hair under a white linen cap, a plain dark shawl over a simple dress; in the visions she glows softly white-gold, mild and loving, taller and more beautiful when the whole bundle burns.

**Supporting.** The boy (ragged, cap, 10 or so); two dark horse-drawn carriages; passers-by and the morning townspeople in period winter coats, bonnets and top hats, faces muted.

**Light as story.** The street is cold and blue; every match brings warm amber light that pushes the blue back; each vision is warmer than the last; the bundle is white-gold, brighter than day; the morning is pale and quiet. Cuts out of a vision land back on the same cold wall.

## Sound

**Narration.** One narrator: a warm, grave, older storyteller's voice (gentle, unhurried, never saccharine), clear English diction. It is the main voice and must be intelligible over everything.

**Dialogue.** The girl: a real child's voice, small and thin with cold; "Matches… who'll buy my matches?" hopeful; "Someone is dying." soft and knowing; "Grandmother! Take me with you!" urgent, tearful. The passer-by: a woman, quiet, more sad than cold: "She wanted to warm herself."

**Score.** Small and intimate: solo piano, celesta or music box, and strings. Cold, sparse opening (street); each match brings a warm, swelling phrase that cuts off when the match dies; a rising, luminous build from the grandmother through the ascent; then a hushed, bare reprise for the morning and a gentle resolution under the end title.

**Effects.** Wind and falling-snow ambience; distant church bells or New Year's bells far off; horses' hooves and carriage wheels rushing past; footsteps in snow; muffled warmth/voices from the lit window; each match strike (a sharp scratch and flare — the text's "Ritsch!"), the flame's soft roar, and its dying; the stove's crackle; the goose's waddle (light, comic); a soft shimmer for the stars and the falling star; a great whoosh of the whole bundle; silence and a single distant bell at dawn.

**Mix.** Narration and dialogue on top; music and effects ducked under speech; stereo; loudness for web playback (see delivery spec).
